<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>PHP memory lab</title>
<style>body{margin:0;background:#101827;color:#eef2ff;font:18px/1.6 system-ui}main{max-width:900px;margin:auto;padding:48px 24px}h1{font-size:48px;line-height:1.1}small,a{color:#a5d9ff}.card{background:#1c2940;padding:24px;border-radius:16px;margin:24px 0}button{padding:12px 18px;border:0;border-radius:8px;background:#9ce6cd;color:#102820;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}select{font:inherit;padding:10px;max-width:100%}.state{border-left:4px solid #9ce6cd;padding:16px;margin-top:16px;min-height:100px}footer{font-size:15px}strong{color:#9ce6cd}</style></head>
<body><main><small>LEARN / PHP2SHELL / OCTOBER 2026</small><h1>When memory outlives its owner.</h1><p>A small Laravel app exploring two PHP engine vulnerabilities. The animation below models memory with text; it does not corrupt memory or send attack requests.</p>
<div class="card"><strong>Runtime: PHP {{ PHP_VERSION }}</strong><p>This app uses a patched PHP runtime. A development server is not a reproduction of Apache mod_php or PHP-FPM.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Step through the failure</h2><label for="bug">Choose a vulnerability</label><br><select id="bug"><option value="oom">CVE-2026-109618: out-of-memory corruption</option><option value="stale">CVE-2026-109619: stale global state</option></select><div class="state" id="state" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><p><button id="next">Next step</button> <button id="reset">Reset</button></p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Why can a memory bug become code execution?</h2><p>PHP's interpreter is native software. If it treats invalid bytes as a trusted internal object, it can make decisions using attacker-influenced data. Depending on the reachable bug and server configuration, that can cause a crash, disclose memory, or redirect execution.</p><p>A successful attacker runs with the PHP worker's permissions. That can expose database credentials, customer data, sessions, or writable site files. It does not automatically grant administrator access to the operating system.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>What this means for WordPress and Laravel</h2><p>Both rely on the PHP engine. Some demonstrated paths execute before application code, so application login and input validation cannot cover them. Server configuration affects exploitability; using PHP alone does not prove a working attack exists against a particular site.</p><p><strong>For site owners:</strong> update PHP, restart all workers, and verify the web-serving runtime. Updating WordPress, plugins, or Composer dependencies alone does not patch PHP.</p><p><strong>For visitors:</strong> PHP runs on the website's server. Visiting a PHP page does not install vulnerable PHP on your computer. A compromised site could still steal information you submit, abuse your session, or serve malicious content.</p></div>
<footer><p>Primary sources: <a href="https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-21-5">PHP security announcement</a>, <a href="https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w">OOM advisory</a>, <a href="https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp">stale-state advisory</a>.</p></footer>
</main><script>
const sequences={oom:['1 / Allocate: PHP starts constructing internal request data. Some fields are not ready yet.','2 / Interrupt: an allocation fails. The old error path abandons initialization while incomplete state remains reachable.','3 / Clean up: shutdown treats that incomplete state as valid. Invalid memory can be read or used.','4 / Consequence: depending on the path and environment, the outcome can include remote code execution. The patch changes out-of-memory handling so shutdown does not process this incomplete state.'],stale:['1 / Request A: a persistent worker keeps a reference to data owned by this request.','2 / End request A: the data is freed, but a missed reset leaves a stale reference. Think of an old room number after the room has been reassigned.','3 / Request B: the same worker accesses the old reference before initialization resets it. The bytes may now belong to something else.','4 / Consequence: interpreting those bytes as the old object can disclose memory or corrupt execution. The fix repairs the lifetime and reset handling.']};let step=0;const bug=document.querySelector('#bug'),state=document.querySelector('#state');function render(){state.textContent=sequences[bug.value][step];document.querySelector('#next').disabled=step===3}document.querySelector('#next').onclick=()=>{step=Math.min(step+1,3);render()};document.querySelector('#reset').onclick=bug.onchange=()=>{step=0;render()};render();
</script></body></html>